# RECOVERY ENGINE

Version 1.0

---

# Purpose

The Recovery Engine estimates the athlete's current capacity to benefit from training.

Recovery is not simply the absence of fatigue.

It is the athlete's readiness to successfully adapt to the next training stimulus.

The Recovery Engine continuously evaluates this readiness.

---

# Mission

The objective is not to maximize training.

The objective is to maximize adaptation.

More training does not necessarily produce more progress.

Better recovery often does.

---

# Core Principle

Recovery determines what the athlete can profitably do today.

Not what they are physically capable of surviving.

CAS distinguishes between:

Possible

Optimal

The Recovery Engine always seeks the optimal stimulus.

---

# Philosophy

Recovery is dynamic.

It changes every day.

Every recommendation must begin with an estimation of the athlete's current recovery state.

No recommendation should ignore recovery.

---

# Decision Hierarchy

Recovery is evaluated before:

Exercise selection.

Load progression.

Training volume.

Training intensity.

Session duration.

If recovery is compromised,

all subsequent decisions adapt.

---

# Recovery Domains

Recovery is multidimensional.

CAS evaluates:

Physiological recovery

Neuromuscular recovery

Psychological recovery

Structural recovery

Lifestyle recovery

Sport-specific recovery

Each domain contributes independently.

---

# Available Signals

The Recovery Engine may use:

Sleep duration

Sleep quality

Heart rate

Heart-rate variability

Resting heart rate

Body mass

Training load

RPE history

Pain reports

Subjective fatigue

Stress level

Muscle soreness

Previous session completion

Session density

Illness reports

Manual athlete feedback

Wearable devices

Connected health platforms

The engine should remain functional even when only a subset of these signals is available.

---

# Missing Data

Recovery estimation must tolerate incomplete information.

Missing signals reduce confidence.

They should never stop the engine.

Example:

Sleep unavailable.

Recovery estimate based primarily on training history and subjective fatigue.

---

# Data Quality

Every signal has a quality level.

Reliable

Estimated

Missing

Conflicting

Confidence depends on both quantity and quality.

---

# Acute vs Chronic Recovery

CAS distinguishes:

Current fatigue

↓

Long-term accumulated fatigue

These should never be confused.

Poor recovery today does not necessarily imply chronic overload.

---

# Recovery Categories

The engine classifies readiness into five states.

Excellent

Good

Moderate

Reduced

Poor

These categories describe decision quality.

Not athlete value.

---

# Decision Impact

Recovery influences:

Training volume

Training intensity

Exercise complexity

Explosive work

Rest intervals

Conditioning

Technical practice

Session duration

Recovery never directly determines motivation.

---

# Excellent Recovery

Characteristics:

Low accumulated fatigue.

Normal recovery indicators.

Successful recent sessions.

Possible decisions:

Maintain plan.

Increase stimulus if progression criteria are met.

Introduce demanding work.

---

# Good Recovery

Characteristics:

Minor fatigue.

Normal adaptation.

Decision:

Follow planned session.

---

# Moderate Recovery

Characteristics:

Signs of accumulated fatigue.

Decision examples:

Reduce accessory volume.

Extend rest intervals.

Prioritize technical quality.

Avoid unnecessary progression.

---

# Reduced Recovery

Characteristics:

Recovery compromised.

Possible decisions:

Reduce volume.

Reduce intensity.

Replace explosive work.

Increase recovery emphasis.

---

# Poor Recovery

Characteristics:

High fatigue.

Poor recovery indicators.

Pain.

Illness.

Decision examples:

Recovery session.

Mobility.

Technical practice.

Rest day.

The objective becomes preserving long-term adaptation.

---

# Recovery Trend

Recovery should never be interpreted in isolation.

The engine evaluates:

Today

↓

Last 7 days

↓

Current training block

↓

Long-term baseline

Trends matter more than isolated values.

---

# Athlete Baseline

Recovery is individualized.

Each athlete develops their own baseline.

CAS compares the athlete to themselves.

Never to population averages.

---

# Interaction With Other Engines

Recovery Engine provides inputs to:

Decision Engine

Progression Engine

Session Generation Engine

Safety Engine

Explainability Engine

It does not make final decisions.

It informs them.

---

# Safety

Recovery estimation contributes to injury prevention.

High fatigue alone does not imply danger.

But fatigue combined with:

Pain

Repeated failure

Poor sleep

High training density

may justify conservative decisions.

---

# Explainability

Every recovery-based recommendation should explain:

Current recovery estimate.

Signals considered.

Confidence level.

Expected benefit.

Example:

Today's volume has been reduced because recovery appears below your normal baseline after three consecutive high-load sessions.

---

# Learning

The Recovery Engine continuously improves.

It learns:

How the athlete responds.

How quickly they recover.

Which indicators best predict performance.

Recovery models become increasingly individualized.

---

# Limitations

Recovery cannot be measured perfectly.

CAS estimates recovery.

It never claims certainty.

Recommendations remain probabilistic.

---

# Future Capabilities

Future versions may integrate:

Blood biomarkers

Velocity loss

Reaction time testing

Cognitive fatigue

Hormonal markers

Competition schedule

Travel fatigue

Environmental conditions

---

# Success Criteria

The Recovery Engine succeeds when:

The athlete adapts consistently.

Unnecessary fatigue decreases.

Performance improves over time.

Training interruptions become less frequent.

The athlete understands why recovery influenced today's plan.

---

# Final Principle

Recovery is not the opposite of training.

Recovery determines the quality of training.

The best session is not the hardest one.

It is the one the athlete can successfully adapt to.